In a nutshell, removing the numbering at the Part portion is easy; I change this entry: {Part 	hesection:}' to {Part:}`. But when I try editing the section portion of my LaTeX template (Eisvogel.tex) and change the subsection to section , and subsubsection to subsection , and so on, it messes things up. %% With Lab, Part, Step and number in section header (Lab Guide Settings) \usepackage{titlesec} 	itleformat{\section} % 	itleformat{\chapter} {
ormalfont\huge fseries\justifyheadingleft\sffamily\color{green}} {Part:}{0.5em}{}[{	itlerule[1.2pt]}] 	itleformat{\section} {
ormalfont\LARGE fseries\justifyheadingright\sffamily\color{green}} {Lab 	hesection:}{0.5em}{}[{	itlerule[1.2pt]}] 	itleformat{\subsection} {
ormalfont\Large fseries\sffamily\color{black}} {Step 	hesubsection}{0em}{} 	itleformat{\subsubsection} {
ormalfont\Large fseries\sffamily\color{black}} {Step 	hesubsubsection}{0em}{} %%% End Lab, Part, and Step section This then generates what looks to be correct, but EVERY Step is now labeled 1.1 .

sed - combine two commands into one command The following two sed commands works. sed -E "s#Example(.*)#
const ex='Example\1';
#; s#Input:(.*)#const\1;#" in.txt | sed '/\S/!d' However, how to rewrite them so that only one sed command is used? i.e. sed -E "???" in.txt I tried this but it did not work: sed -E "s#Example(.*)#
const ex='Example\1';
#; s#Input:(.*)#const\1;#; /\S/!d" in.txt in.txt: Example 1: Input: s = "()" GNU sed version is 4.7. Expected output: const ex='Example 1:'; const s = "()";
